            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical prophecy, 'islands' or 'coastlands' primarily serve as a geographic metonymy for distant Gentile nations or the remote ends of the earth. In apocalyptic contexts, they represent stable elements of creation that are shaken or removed in divine judgment.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"An island represents a set-apart people who emerge from the sea of nations, awaiting God's law and experiencing preservation while surrounding kingdoms fall.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Distant Gentile nations, remote coastlands, or geographical extremes of the earth.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A set-apart, preserved spiritual people (the remnant or church) standing amidst the sea of nations.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus views the term geographically and ethnically as remote Gentile territories. The book completely rejects this geographic metonymy in favor of a conceptual allegory where the 'island' is God's separated people and the surrounding 'sea' is the nations.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies islands as unreached, far-off Gentiles who will eventually hear of God's salvation. The book identifies islands typologically as the already set-apart people (the righteous remnant or church) preserved from the surrounding chaos of the nations.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book adopts a rare allegorical interpretation that redefines the referent from distant geographical Gentile nations to a spiritually separated people, fundamentally contradicting the established prophetic metonymy recognized by the consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In Isaiah, the 'islands' explicitly refer to distant Gentile peoples who are being summoned to hear God's law, not a previously set-apart people. Furthermore, in Revelation 16:20, islands fleeing away signifies the catastrophic destruction of earthly stability under God's wrath, which directly conflicts with the book's claim that islands are 'preserved while surrounding kingdoms fall.'\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
